    "case_summary": "A 53-year-old man with history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia, diabetes mellitus type 2, heart failure with recovered ejection fraction presented to the emergency department with left arm and left leg weakness that began 90 minutes before. Before presentation, the patient had sneezed and felt pain throughout the head then developed tingling and weakness in the left arm and left leg. Five months previously, the patient was hospitalized with cardiac issues, and cardiac magnetic resonance imaging showed an ejection fraction of 30% with global hypokinesis. The patient underwent CABG with Impella 5.5 support and was discharged home on postoperative day 7.\n\nVital signs on admission:- Blood pressure: 146/85 mm Hg- Heart rate: 87 beats/min- Oxygen saturation: 97% on room air- Respiratory rate: 18 breaths/minNational Institute of Health Stroke Scale score was 2.\n\n- Imaging Studies:  1. CT scan of head and CT angiography of head and neck: negative for any acute findings  2. CT angiography of chest: revealed an intraluminal clot in the thoracic aorta     Image Title: CT angiography of chest (cross-sectional view)     Image Description: Shows an intraluminal clot in the thoracic aorta     Image Title: CT angiography of chest (coronal view)     Image Description: Shows an intraluminal clot in thoracic aorta  3. MRI of brain: showed multiple acute embolic infarcts     Image Title: MRI scan of brain     Image Description: Shows embolic pattern of acute small and punctate acute infarcts",
